### Description

[`insert()`](https://json.nlohmann.me/api/basic_json/insert/#exception-safety) documents for all five overloads: *"Strong exception safety: if an exception occurs, the original value stays intact."* That does not hold when an allocation fails while the elements are copied: the value is modified, and with `JSON_DIAGNOSTICS` the parent pointers of the surviving elements are left stale, so the next operation that checks the invariant aborts. Three related symptoms:

**(1) Object range insert keeps the members inserted before the throw.** `insert(first, last)` for objects calls `m_data.m_value.object->insert(first, last)` directly ([json.hpp#L5087](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L5087)); `std::map::insert(first, last)` and `ordered_map::insert` only give the basic guarantee. With `{"a":1}` and a three-member source, a `bad_alloc` on the 4th allocation leaves `{"a":1,"m":"xxx…"}` (libc++ and libstdc++; `ordered_json` at k = 8).

**(2) Array inserts with a throwing element copy modify the array.** `insert(pos, const basic_json&)`, `insert(pos, cnt, val)` and `insert(pos, first, last)` forward to `std::vector::insert` in `insert_iterator()` ([json.hpp#L4921-L4927](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L4921-L4927)). The standard gives `vector::insert` the "no effects" guarantee only for a single element at the end when the element's copy constructor throws, and `basic_json`'s copy constructor allocates for strings and containers. Observed:

| overload | capacity | libc++ (Apple clang 21) | libstdc++ (GCC 16) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `insert(pos, val)` in the middle | spare (as after any `erase()`) | `[1,2,3]` → `[1,null,2,3]` | intact |
| `insert(pos, 3, val)` in the middle | spare | three `null`s inserted | three `null`s inserted |
| `insert(pos, first, last)` in the middle | spare | `null`s inserted | `null`s inserted |
| `insert(pos, first, last)` in the middle | **none** (reallocation) | intact | **first element lost**: `["…1","…2",…]` → `[null,"…2",…]` (libstdc++ moves the prefix into the new buffer before copying the range) |
| `insert(end(), 3, val)` | spare | part of the count appended | part of the count appended |

`insert(pos, basic_json&&)` and `insert(pos, ilist)` are fine: they build the elements first and insert by move (the `ilist` overload already uses a local `array_t values` for that reason).

**(3) Stale parent pointers under `JSON_DIAGNOSTICS`.** `set_parents()` runs only after the container operation succeeded ([json.hpp#L4934](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L4934), [#L5088](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a652cd098dd78225d80f1909b921871/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L5088)). After (1) or (2) threw, the elements the container moved have `m_parent == nullptr` (the move constructor does not copy it) or point to their old slot. The next `assert_invariant()` aborts a debug build (e.g. `json copy = j;`), and in release builds the diagnostic paths in exception messages are wrong.

**Since when.** All of 3.x; (1) and (2) reproduce unchanged with the 3.12.0 single header (same output as below). The documentation sentence is from 2018 (`insert.md` history).

**Possible fix.** For the array overloads, copy the new elements into a local `array_t` first (as `insert(pos, ilist)` does) and insert with `std::make_move_iterator`; since `basic_json`'s move constructor is `noexcept`, `vector::insert` then has no effects on an allocation failure. For the object range, insert into a copy of the object and swap, or erase the members inserted so far on exception (only those that were new). In both cases call `set_parents()` from a cleanup path (e.g. a `JSON_TRY`/`JSON_CATCH` that rethrows, or a scope guard) so that the invariant holds after a throw. If the strong guarantee is not wanted for these overloads, `insert.md` has to say so; note that about 23 other API pages carry the same sentence and a fail-point sweep found it to hold for all of them (`push_back`, `emplace`, `emplace_back`, `operator+=`, `operator[]` with index, key and `json_pointer`, `operator=`, `erase`, `swap`, `patch`, `flatten`/`unflatten`, `diff`, converting constructors, `to_json`, `from_*`; `update`/`merge_patch` document the basic guarantee, which holds). Regression tests belong next to the `controlled bad_alloc` cases in `tests/src/unit-allocator.cpp`; that file has no `insert` coverage today.

Related, not duplicates: #5652/#5695 (`operator>>` left a partial value that broke the diagnostics invariant), #5640 (deep copy under `bad_alloc`), #5940 (strong guarantee for `operator[](json_pointer)`).

### Reproduction steps

1. Save the programs below as `r2.cpp`, `r3.cpp` and `r4.cpp`.
2. `clang++ -std=c++17 -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined -I include r2.cpp -o r2 && ./r2` (same for `r3`; `r4` needs `-DJSON_DIAGNOSTICS=1` and assertions enabled, i.e. no `-DNDEBUG`).

Each program replaces `operator new` so that the `k`-th allocation throws `std::bad_alloc` and compares `dump()` before and after the failed `insert()`.

### Expected vs. actual results

Expected: after a `bad_alloc` from `insert()`, `j.dump()` is unchanged and `j` stays usable.

Actual:

- `r2`: `k=4: bad_alloc, value changed` — before `{"a":1}`, after `{"a":1,"m":"xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"}`
- `r3`: `k=1: bad_alloc, value changed` — before `[1,2,3]`, after `[1,null,2,3]`
- `r4`: prints `after failed insert: [1,null,[2,3],4]`, then `Assertion failed: (!check_parents || !is_structured() || std::all_of(begin(), end(), [this](const basic_json & j) { return j.m_parent == this; })), function assert_invariant, file json.hpp, line 1081.`

### Minimal code example

Common prologue for all three:

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <new>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
using json = nlohmann::json;
static long counter = 0, fail_at = 0;
void* operator new(std::size_t n)
{
    if (fail_at > 0 && ++counter == fail_at) throw std::bad_alloc();
    void* p = std::malloc(n ? n : 1); if (!p) throw std::bad_alloc(); return p;
}
void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
```

`r2.cpp` — object range insert:

```cpp
int main()
{
    const std::string L(64, 'x');
    const json source = {{"m", L}, {"n", L}, {"o", L}};
    for (long k = 1; k < 100; ++k)
    {
        json j = {{"a", 1}};
        const std::string before = j.dump();
        counter = 0; fail_at = k;
        try { j.insert(source.begin(), source.end()); fail_at = 0; std::printf("k=%ld: no exception\n", k); return 0; }
        catch (const std::bad_alloc&) { fail_at = 0; }
        if (j.dump() != before) { std::printf("k=%ld: bad_alloc, value changed\n  before %s\n  after  %s\n", k, before.c_str(), j.dump().c_str()); return 1; }
    }
}
```

`r3.cpp` — array insert with spare capacity (libc++):

```cpp
int main()
{
    const json val = std::string(64, 'x');     // copying it allocates
    for (long k = 1; k < 100; ++k)
    {
        json j = {1, 2, 3};
        j.push_back(4); j.erase(3);            // spare capacity: the insert below does not reallocate
        const std::string before = j.dump();
        counter = 0; fail_at = k;
        try { j.insert(j.begin() + 1, val); fail_at = 0; std::printf("k=%ld: no exception\n", k); return 0; }
        catch (const std::bad_alloc&) { fail_at = 0; }
        if (j.dump() != before) { std::printf("k=%ld: bad_alloc, value changed\n  before %s\n  after  %s\n", k, before.c_str(), j.dump().c_str()); return 1; }
    }
}
```

`r4.cpp` — stale parents, build with `-DJSON_DIAGNOSTICS=1`:

```cpp
int main()
{
    const json val = std::string(64, 'x');
    json j = {1, {2, 3}, 4};
    j.push_back(5); j.erase(3);                // spare capacity
    counter = 0; fail_at = 1;                  // the first allocation (the copy of val) fails
    try { j.insert(j.begin() + 1, val); } catch (const std::bad_alloc&) { fail_at = 0; }
    std::printf("after failed insert: %s\n", j.dump().c_str());
    json copy = j;                             // assert_invariant() checks the parents
    std::printf("copy ok\n");
}
```

For the libstdc++ element loss, replace the array in `r3` by five long strings, drop the `push_back`/`erase` line and insert a range: `j.insert(j.begin() + 1, other.begin(), other.end())` with `other` an array of long strings; k = 2 then prints `after [null,"…2",…]` with GCC 16.

### Error messages

See *Expected vs. actual results*. No sanitizer report; the values are valid but changed, and `r4` aborts on the assertion quoted above.

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.3.34.2), libc++, macOS 27; GCC 16.1, libstdc++, Debian (Docker `gcc:16`).

### Library version

develop at ddc34f546a (2026-11-01) and 3.12.0.

### Validation

- [x] The bug also occurs if the latest version from the [`develop`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/tree/develop) branch is used.
- [x] I can successfully [compile and run the unit tests](https://github.com/nlohmann/json#execute-unit-tests).

---
*Found by an allocation fail-point sweep during a bug hunt; written by Claude Code on behalf of @nlohmann.*
